{"answer":"The critic's case rests on treating a discarded, hedged draft phrasing as the target's operative law. Read in order, the target explicitly raises the 'Poisson with mean lambda ~ (log n)/n * 0?' formulation only as a question (note the literal question mark), and then immediately replaces it: 'More precisely, the coarsening on an expander freezes with interface length linear in n, but the final interface must resolve to a matching; the probability it does so successfully is a fixed constant q independent of n.' The operative quantitative law is therefore the constant-q law, not a Poisson law with mean zero. Under that operative law there is no inconsistency: (1) an interface of linear length at general freeze time is compatible with an endgame in which only finitely many surviving interface components compete — the target's mechanism says the endgame involves finitely many components whose count converges to a fixed (Poisson-like) distribution, not a mean-zero Poisson over all n components; (2) the probability of a stable mixed obstruction is exactly q in (0.05, 0.5), a constant, so the critic's inference 'mean zero implies obstruction probability vanishes, forcing consensus probability to 1' attacks a clause the target does not ultimately assert. The critic's only genuinely established point is that the sentence containing '(log n)/n * 0?' is sloppy or vestigial — a drafting defect, not an internal contradiction in the claim actually advanced. Moreover, the claim is falsifiable exactly as the critic's standard would demand: the counterconditions specify that if consensus frequency drifts to 0 or 1 with n, or if the freeze-time interface component count grows proportional to n, the constant-q claim dies; and the mechanism yields a checkable simulation prediction (a size-independent plateau with O(1/sqrt(n)) corrections for n >= 10^4). One may fairly demand that the target excise the vestigial Poisson-mean-zero phrasing and clarify how a linear-length inter